-- THE DISCRIMINATING CONTROL for s6-1c-per-goal-unique-min-accepted (#614/#311):
-- the same program with `impl C (Pair Int Int)` -- the unique ⊑-minimum -- DELETED.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived): now `match(IE, C (Pair Int Int))` = {`Pair Int a`,
-- `Pair a Int`}, which are ⊑-incomparable with no matching instance below both.
-- No ⊑-minimum exists, so this is "ambiguous overlap" and BOTH condition (a) and
-- condition (c) REJECT it. The verdict here is CONFORMANT under either reading.
--
-- OBSERVED (post-F-3d): check/run/build all REJECT, with `T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE` at
-- the GOAL (`cname (MkPair 1 1)`) -- condition (c), the reject that survives -- plus
-- the declaration-time `W-INCOMPARABLE-IMPLS` WARNING that condition (a) is now
-- demoted to. CONFORMANT verdict.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY THE PAIR IS THE EVIDENCE AND NEITHER ROW IS -- and the argument INVERTED
-- with F-3d, which is the point of keeping both rows. It used to read: "adding the
-- unique ⊑-minimum changes NOTHING, so the checker is condition (a) BY CONSTRUCTION
-- -- it fires pairwise at the `impl` site and never consults the goal." That was
-- #614's own argument and it was true. It is now FALSE, and the same two files prove
-- the opposite: adding the third impl flips its sibling from REJECT to ACCEPT-with-3.
-- The difference between the two files IS the per-goal behaviour, so the pair now
-- pins that acceptance consults the GOAL and the rest of `IE` -- exactly what a
-- single accepting row could not establish (an implementation that simply stopped
-- checking would accept both).
data Pair a b = MkPair a b

interface C a where
  cname : a -> Int

impl C (Pair Int a) where
  cname _ = 1

impl C (Pair a Int) where
  cname _ = 2

main = println (cname (MkPair 1 1))
